MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad496817cc2ac7b498b32f0fc2de6fb3, type: 3}
  m_Name: R1_03_TogetherWeMove
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::JournalEntry
  title: "3 \xB7 Together We Move"
  pages:
  - bigIdea: Forces on the same object add up. Two small forces can beat one big
      friction.
    notes: The causeway collapsed long ago; only the old cargo ferry still crosses
      the acid. It sails when its deck carries a heavy load. The stone crate is too
      heavy for either of us alone. But a push and a pull at the same time, in the
      same direction, add together.
    lookCloser: "Friction on 3 kg: \u03BC\xB7m\xB7g = <color=#7d3029>29.4 N</color>\nPush
      <color=#a26029>12 N</color> alone: no.   Tether <color=#267682>24 N</color>
      alone: no.\nPush + tether = <color=#a26029>12</color> + <color=#267682>24</color>
      = 36 N \u2192 a = (36 \u2212 29.4) \xF7 3 = 2.2 m/s\xB2"
    funFact: 'Engineers call adding forces like this a "vector sum": direction matters
      as much as size.'
    marginP1: I push, you pull. Same way!
    marginP2: Pulling from the front still counts.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        3 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Tether
        newtons: 24
        direction: 0
        kind: 1
        unit: N
      - label: Push
        newtons: 12
        direction: 0
        kind: 0
        unit: N
      - label: Friction
        newtons: 29.4
        direction: 1
        kind: 2
        unit: N
      caption: '12 + 24 = 36 N forward against 29.4 N: it moves.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: 'Inertia rides along: things keep doing what they were doing until a
      force changes it.'
    notes: Watch the crate on the ferry. When the ferry starts, the crate lags behind
      for a moment; when it stops, the crate keeps sliding forward. Only friction
      from the deck drags it along. Keep cargo away from the edges!
    lookCloser: "Friction on the 3 kg crate: up to <color=#7d3029>29.4 N</color>,
      so a = 29.4 \xF7 3 = 9.8 m/s\xB2\nFerry speed 2 m/s: the crate catches up in
      2 \xF7 9.8 \u2248 0.2 s\nand slips about v\xB2 \xF7 (2a) = 4 \xF7 19.6 \u2248
      0.2 m each time."
    funFact: 'It''s why a bus pushes you back when it starts and forward when it
      brakes. You aren''t pushed: you just keep going.'
    marginP1: The crate didn't move. The ferry did.
    marginP2: Then the crate wanted to keep going.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        3 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Friction
        newtons: 29.4
        direction: 0
        kind: 2
        unit: N
      caption: As the ferry starts right, friction is the only force pulling the
        crate along.
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  tip: 'The ferry sails when it carries at least 85 N of cargo (people don''t count),
    and waits a moment so you can hop on. The stone crate is too heavy for one of
    you: push and pull it together. At the far side, climb the crate.'
  sketch: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 005e215e7c4403447aa9ab1b37d76ffa, type: 3}
  sketchCaption: 'FLIP: each one reverses it.'
